WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:02.200
What?

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:03.000
Where am I?

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.720
Phoenix, Arizona, at night.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:13.240
Did you seriously just kidnap me and drive me to the middle of the desert for a "Dark Phoenix" pun?

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:17.180
Oh, I did much more than that Matpat.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.500
So much more, haha!

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.840
MOUSE!

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.100
Hello internet! Welcome to Film Theory!

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.680
The show that's awash in the blood of metahumans.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:35.309
Seriously, at this point, dead superheroes are to Film Theory what dead kids are to Game Theory.

00:00:35.309 --> 00:00:40.289
But today, I thought we'd look at a little less literal of a killing and more of a metaphorical one.

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.040
Let's talk X-Men's "Dark Phoenix",

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.820
a film that came out to the worst reviews in the franchise's history,

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:50.300
and has grossly underperformed at the box office,

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.520
which honestly is really sad to me.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:53.900
Without the original X-Men film,

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.840
superhero movies just wouldn't be where they are today.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:00.000
The one-two combo of X-Men and then Sam Raimi's Spider-Man,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.720
proved that a comic book hero's most powerful ability was the power to sell tickets.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.820
The new movies have been really solid.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.628
"First Class" and "Days of Future Past" were amazing-

00:01:09.628 --> 00:01:11.140
(ignores "X-Men: Apocalypse")

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.900
Like I said, *those two* were amazing,

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.369
and with "Dark Phoenix" being the last in the series now that Disney's in charge,

00:01:17.369 --> 00:01:21.779
it's a bummer to see such a pivotal franchise go out not with the bang, but with a whimper.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:25.259
So today I wanted to look at what went wrong with this new movie.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:30.640
I mean, its opening weekend earned half of even the worst movies of the "X-Men" franchise.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.160
That is serious.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:35.800
When you're doing worse than "X-Men Origins: Wolverine", you know you done goofed.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:38.220
And admittedly there's a lot of blame to go around,

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:42.600
Sophie Turner struggling to maintain an American accent while saying the word "father",

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.340
Michael Fassbender being just as confused as to why Magneto's in this movie as the audience is,

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:53.360
the marketing team deciding not to put the words "X-Men" at the front of the movie's title in giant bold letters.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.000
Guys, an "X" with a circle is just not gonna cut it when it comes to brand awareness.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.000
I saw that and thought it was telling me that this was a movie about railroad crossings.

00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:04.560
But those are all just the minor points.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.369
Well, not that last one, I actually think that last one is a really big misstep.

00:02:08.369 --> 00:02:12.120
You don't know this is an "X-Men" movie unless you know what Dark Phoenix is referencing.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.060
Still, the biggest blame of all goes to one villain in particular.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:18.700
Forget Mr Sinister, Apocalypse and Juggernaut,

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:24.120
the entity that's now proven itself to be the greatest enemy to the X-Men is Captain Marvel.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:28.170
But to really understand why, and why to a level that no one else is covering,

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:30.839
let me first quickly recap both movies to you,

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.539
and then, things will start to make a little bit more sense

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:38.840
Captain Marvel: Set in the early 90's, a strong independent female named Carol Danvers,

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:44.660
is in a space related accident with an experimental aircraft that results in her absorbing cosmic powers.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:47.440
The accident also gives her sudden onset amnesia.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:51.260
Her mentor actively lies to her about her past, but she learns the truth,

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:53.360
which in turn unleashes her true potential,

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.380
and she turns on the man who was once her teacher,

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.120
setting out on a massive mission of revenge.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:03.180
Oh, and there happens to be a race of shape-shifting aliens called the Skrulls that are caught in the crossfire.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:05.740
"Dark Phoenix" Set in the early 90's,

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:08.160
a strong independent female named Jean Grey

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.560
is in a space related accident with an experimental aircraft that results in her absorbing cosmic powers.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.860
Though she doesn't experience any memory loss,

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:22.000
it's eventually revealed that her mentor has been actively lying to her about her past since she was a little girl.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.620
She learns the truth, which unleashes her true potential.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:26.720
She turns on the man who was once her teacher,

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:29.220
setting out on a massive mission of self-discovery,

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:34.160
oh and, uh, there's a race of shape-shifting aliens called the D'Bari caught in a crossfire.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.440
"Captain Marvel" released into theaters March 8th 2019,

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.920
"Dark Phoenix" was supposed to release November 2nd of 2018,

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.980
then again on February 14th of this year,

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:48.450
months and then mere weeks earlier than "Captain Marvel".

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:51.239
Are you starting to notice a problem here?

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:56.279
Okay, so admittedly I may have oversimplified the plots of these two movies just a wee little bit,

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:00.120
But there is very clearly a bit of overlap between the two films.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.880
Female superheroes with space powers and counter shape-shifting aliens,

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:06.140
and deal with their lying mentor,

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.660
and here I thought releasing biopics of famous musical artists,

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:10.460
named after their most iconic songs,

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:12.620
within a six-month span was a bad idea

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:16.340
But in all seriousness, the overlap was originally gonna be greater.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:17.680
Much greater!

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.800
You see, "Dark Phoenix" currently ends with the battle on a train,

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:25.200
but was originally meant to end with a battle in space.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.480
Just like the ending of another movie- "Captain Marvel".

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:32.020
According to leaked reports off of test screenings for the film,

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:36.720
the third act of "Dark Phoenix" culminated in a full-scale alien invasion at the U.N.,

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.020
where Jean Grey has to launch herself into space to defeat the alien armada.

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:44.140
Hmmm, where have I seen that before?

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:52.020
In the end, Jean sacrifices herself to destroy the fleet.

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:55.480
It's something that appears to be confirmed by Simon Kinberg,

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.740
the writer, director and producer of "Dark Phoenix".

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:03.640
"My original ending didn't have the entire X-family together the way they are in the film now."

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:08.420
"Usually, these big, huge action movies have the climactic moment in the third act."

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:11.680
"I loved the way that "Civil War" had its big action set piece,"

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.300
"where everyone's facing off more towards the end of the second act, rather than in the third,"

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:21.240
"so that after that huge battle, you're left with Winter Soldier, Captain America and Iron Man."

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:26.000
"It's this telescoped down view of their raw emotions, and I loved how intimate that was."

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:33.740
"That's what I was going for with "Dark Phoenix" ending, even though it then might have looked like Captain Marvel for about two minutes."

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:37.840
So basically, Jean sacrifices herself and the team winds up divided.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:39.550
That's how I read into that quote,

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:44.540
but in the process, as he admits, it looks like a Captain Marvel space battle for two minutes.

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:45.910
So what's the big deal here?

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:49.480
It's only like two minutes of similarities between the two movies, right?

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:53.960
Well, if you calculate the time that Captain Marvel is actually in space,

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:57.310
doing battle against an armada of invading alien ships,

00:05:57.310 --> 00:06:01.959
it winds up being you guessed it- almost exactly two minutes.

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:08.500
Okay, we have two movies, with strong females, getting accidental cosmic superpowers,

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:10.120
while also being lied to by their mentors,

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:12.677
leading them to ultimately encounter shape-shifting aliens,

00:06:12.677 --> 00:06:16.329
and have a climactic space battle against an armada of ships for exactly two minutes,

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:17.440
But come on!

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:20.680
Those sorts of coincidences happen everyday in Hollywood!

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:25.449
It's not like the X-Men and Captain Marvel are dealing with the same aliens invading Earth, right?

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:28.660
Ha, one are the D'Bari and one are the Skrulls and Kree,

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:29.780
They're not the same, right?

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:30.520
Right?

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:32.470
They're the same freaking aliens, aren't they?

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:32.950
Yep!

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:35.660
The D'Bari, who end up as the alien race in "Dark Phoenix",

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:38.460
and who go unnamed for practically the entire movie,

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:41.120
(which should  already tip you off that this was changed during reshoots)

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:43.460
were originally meant to be the Skrulls.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:48.360
In fact, in the comics, the D'Bari basically get one panel in the Dark Phoenix saga,

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.420
in which they're being wiped out by Jean Grey.

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:51.009
And that's it.

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:53.520
That's all the characterization they ever get.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:57.320
Not much to build an entire villain civilization around for your new movie, FOX.

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:01.209
for further proof that these shape shifters were originally meant to be the Skrulls,

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:04.840
we turn to an interview with the actor of Cyclops, Tye Sheridan.

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:09.280
"There's this huge battle between the guards at the U.N. and Jean Grey,"

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:11.380
"and all the guards turn out to be Skrulls,"

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:16.360
"and then Jean and Scott are - Scott is fighting Skrulls in the fountain."

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:22.340
And for further proof, we turn to the head Skrull- I mean, sorry, head D'Bari herself, Jessica Chastain,

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:23.860
who had this to say about her character.

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:26.200
"So it was always, like, everyday, kind of trying to figure out,

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:27.260
"Who am I?"

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:29.880
"You know, the mystery of "Who is this character?"

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:33.010
"and then understanding that, also with the reshoots, you kind of go,"

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:34.358
"Oh, it's changing again."

00:07:34.358 --> 00:07:37.280
"You know, I mean, it was like a constant evolution."

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:43.560
So, how was "Dark Phoenix" expecting to get away with using the Skrulls when it's clearly a Marvel property?

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:47.780
Well, in truth, the question should actually be asked the other way.

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:53.200
Many fans had assumed that the Skrulls couldn't appear in the MCU because of their connection to the Fantastic Four,

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:56.460
a Marvel property whose film rights were sold to FOX,

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:58.900
back in the days when Marvel was bankrupt.

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:00.300
Ha, Marvel being bankrupt.

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:04.040
Man, if at first you don't succeed, sell sell sell to Disney.

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:07.599
But seriously, the Skrulls first appeared in Fantastic Four #2,

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:11.400
and then again in Fantastic Four #18, that was their origin.

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:14.540
Seems like it should be a slam dunk for "Dark Phoenix" to use the property,

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:17.520
except, like all things in life, it's more complicated than that.

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:20.380
According to a tweet from "Guardians of the Galaxy" director James Gunn,

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:24.860
The Skrulls actually have shared custody between Marvel and FOX.

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:31.040
"Some specific Skrulls are at FOX. The scrolls as a whole are co-owned."

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:34.366
So, apparently Marvel gets custody Wednesdays and every other weekend.

00:08:34.366 --> 00:08:37.940
But in all seriousness, how did these two movies end up like this?

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:43.300
The "Dark Phoenix" saga is, at its core, a very different story from "Captain Marvel".

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:49.440
In the X-Men, "Dark Phoenix" is all about a team of mutants being forced to stop one of their own from turning evil.

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:52.140
Captain Marvel, meanwhile, is the exact opposite,

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:56.431
The story of a solo pilot in a cosmic case of Stockholm Syndrome.

00:08:56.431 --> 00:08:57.520
So what happened?

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:00.580
Well James McAvoy, the actor who plays Professor X,

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:02.600
may have summed up the situation best.

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:06.980
"We're trawling through the same source material, it seems."

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:10.960
Obviously, the "Dark Phoenix" saga and "Captain Marvel" aren't the same comic,

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:13.780
but in a lot of ways, they may as well be.

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:16.780
Before the X-Men were canceled in 1970-

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:18.300
Yeah, that's another surprise!

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:21.420
It was such an unpopular comic when it was first released,

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:23.780
that it was cancelled for half a decade in the 70's!

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:27.720
Anyway back then, Jean Grey often went by the moniker "Marvel Girl",

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:31.419
and Captain Marvel is just Marvel Girl after a few promotions, right?

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:33.669
No, obviously, I'm just joking,

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:36.640
but the two do still share a remarkable heritage.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:38.979
When Jean was brought back for the "Dark Phoenix" saga,

00:09:38.980 --> 00:09:41.620
it was by comic writer Chris Claremont.

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:43.280
Claremont took Jean Grey,

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:45.940
who at the time was considered a throwaway character,

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:47.440
and made her a star.

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:49.620
I mean literally he made her a star,

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:51.360
but Claremont's career as a comic writer,

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:53.980
and reviver of throwaway characters was far from over.

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:58.620
Fast forward a few years, and Claremont is working on a comic called Ms.Marvel.

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:02.649
Maybe tempted to believe that this is an evolution of Marvel Girl with a bit more maturity,

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:05.680
But actually Ms. Marvel was an entirely different character.

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:08.620
Not Jean Grey, but Carol Danvers.

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:13.720
Carol would decades later take on the mantle of her predecessor and become Captain Marvel,

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:19.040
but only after Claremont's lobbying for his female spin-off to take over for the traditionally male role.

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:24.280
Both characters revived and molded in huge ways by Claremont.

00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:28.200
And so in a lot of ways, the source material really was the same.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:29.530
Even all that being considered though,

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:34.580
obviously, the impending acquisition of FOX by Disney didn't help things along.

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:39.520
Captain Marvel may not have killed Jean Grey, but The Mouse certainly did.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:42.360
First, we saw what they did with the  Skrull in Captain Marvel,

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:46.040
much to everyone's surprise, they're the good guys in that movie.

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:49.180
Would be confusing for audiences to see them acting good in one movie,

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:53.000
and then suddenly turning heel in the next that happens only a month later.

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:57.310
Or worse for Disney, Dark Phoenix releasing before Captain Marvel,

00:10:57.310 --> 00:11:01.160
and now the MCU's big female hero flick gets railroaded,

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:05.880
as audiences grapple with the inconsistent Skrull treatment, and "Dark Phoenix" gets there first.

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:09.670
The Mouse sacrificed Jean Grey for Carol Danvers.

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:11.360
This was honestly crucial,

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:15.020
since Disney clearly has plans for the Skrulls moving forward,

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:18.340
They know where Marvel's headed into the post-"Endgame" phase of movie,

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:22.360
and the Skrulls are 99% certain to factor into that.

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:24.740
Kevin Feige, the mastermind behind the MCU,

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:30.780
has been very upfront when speaking about a branching division of Earth-centric films and space-centric films,

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:33.920
and with that comes the continuation of the story of the Skrull,

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:38.260
as they are heavily involved in some of the most iconic Marvel stories out there,

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:44.050
specifically "Secret Invasion", where fan favorite characters are suddenly replaced by doppelganger Skrulls.

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:47.060
That is an impossible plotline to pass up,

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:51.840
but two movies using the same characters would have just caused both of those movies,

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:54.720
and specifically that race of characters to suffer.

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:57.140
And so, just like a divorce with joint custody,

00:11:57.140 --> 00:11:59.740
the primary caregiver is the one who gets the say.

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:03.160
And in this case, we're all forced to bow to the will of Disney

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:08.260
Especially a struggling studio that's looking to get a 71 billion dollar buyout, FOX.

00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:09.540
And that's kind of sad,

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:14.740
as we're going forward into a world where Disney is going to own all but the DC Superheroes,

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:17.280
we may want to pause at this moment and think about what this really means.

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:21.920
Sure, it means awesome things, like Wolverine and Hulk finally sharing the screen together,

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:25.720
But it also means no more FOX taking chances, and doing things differently.

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:30.720
No more baffling "Fantastic Four" movies, as they desperately try to hold on to the IP,

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:33.780
giving us movies that we can laugh at for decades to come.

00:12:34.029 --> 00:12:36.729
No more Universal giving comic book movies to Ang Lee.

00:12:36.729 --> 00:12:40.460
But with two or three movies coming out each year into the foreseeable future,

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:45.000
Disney is gonna have to start to learn that some things are just gonna have to overlap,

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:48.480
or we may end up with a lot more Dark Phoenixes on our hands.

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:51.370
But hey, that's just a theory!

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:54.000
A Film Theory!

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.240
Aaaaand cut.

